<programme channel="DocuBox.HD.fr" start="20261017162500 +0000" stop="20261017165000 +0000">
    <title lang="fr">Quirky Science</title>
    <sub-title lang="fr">Rubber</sub-title>
    <desc lang="fr">Rubber was first used by South American tribes for waterproofing and bouncing balls. Westerners struggled with melting rubber until Charles Goodyear discovered vulcanization, making rubber durable. This sparked the rubber boom, enriching cities like Manaus. Britain broke Brazil's monopoly by smuggling seeds. Later, synthetic rubber and Kevlar were developed—Kevlar is so tough it can stop bullets. Rubber's journey is full of quirky discoveries.</desc>
    <category lang="fr">Documentaire scientifique</category>
    <icon src="http://static-cdn.tv.sfr.net/data/img/pl/3/7/9/15271973.jpg" />
    <episode-num system="onscreen">E6</episode-num>
  </programme>
  
<programme channel="DocuBox.HD.fr" start="20261017165000 +0000" stop="20261017171500 +0000">
    <title lang="fr">Quirky Science</title>
    <sub-title lang="fr">Canning</sub-title>
    <desc lang="fr">Canned food began with Napoleon's contest to preserve food, won by Appert's boiled, sealed bottles. The British later developed metal cans, though early ones exploded or caused illness. Can openers came 30 years later. Pasteur's germ theory explained spoilage. Pressure-cooking improved taste, and now, 'raddaperization'—zapping food—is the future. Canning evolved from battlefield necessity to high-tech preservation.</desc>
    <category lang="fr">Documentaire scientifique</category>
    <icon src="http://static-cdn.tv.sfr.net/data/img/pl/3/7/9/15271973.jpg" />
    <episode-num system="onscreen">E7</episode-num>
  </programme>
  
